  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the locker room, Fedya checks on Senya who is feeling very sick. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you all right?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I am very bad.' Senya links 'no' directly with 'to me' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you.' The verb for 'said' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not eat that fish.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'eat' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I thought she was normal.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'normal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you drink charcoal?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'was drinking' is enough to make it a question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, I ate two packs.' Notice how 'yeah' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'devour'. Then Fedya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No shit.' Fedya links 'not a single' directly with 'horseradish' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My stomach hurts.' Senya combines 'at' with 'belly' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to go home.' Russian builds this impersonally with the dative ('to me' or 'to you') and 'home'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot work today.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'can' to negate the action. Then Fedya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go home and rest.' Notice how 'go' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'rest'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
